From 9b3b7de52b2050efbb11671c33ee9ad0a7094c2f Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: daveey <daveey@users.noreply.github.com>
Date: Thu, 19 Nov 2026 08:07:42 +0000
Subject: [PATCH] fix(accounting): name direct pool submissions without
 duplicate scopes [daveey]

Why: idle native snapshots attribute CPU to concurrent.futures.thread._worker,
not the submitted function. Existing asyncio instrumentation misses direct
ThreadPoolExecutor.submit calls. Physical-work custody is not CPU accounting,
and context/custody wrappers can hide the original callable.

Instrument the public submit boundary, retain the logical target through
partial/context/custody carriers, and make layered thread scopes idempotent.
Keep execution through the original custody/context wrapper. Daemon pool
submissions retain per-submission context with an inspectable partial.

Name Context.run thread targets by their original callable. Fold exited
native IDs before replacement targets begin and at final booking: exit-only
folding leaves a stale nested-scope baseline and double-books inner CPU.

Proof: original pool RED 7 failed/2 passed; thread-followup RED 2 failed;
nested-reuse RED 1 failed/26 passed. Expanded GREEN 125 passed, 2 skipped;
prior broader importing-consumer run 372 passed, 13 skipped. Full scripts/check
passed. Scope/custody matrix includes active ownership, exception identity,
context changes, repeated submissions, disabled scopes and hook restoration.
Existing physical-lifetime tests now also run with executor hooks enabled.
Mutation baseline: 28 passed; all 6 targeted mutants killed, no survivors.
Final restored-tree full scripts/check --base origin/main passed.

This is attribution, not a claimed live CPU reduction. Native idle Mac
measurement and any measured >=1%-core caller optimization remain owned by
t_84a411c80a478c57; no live restart/update is part of this patch.
